The fix works as required.

## Summary

The bug was in `src/utils/deepEqual.ts`. When comparing an empty array `[]` with an empty plain object `{}`, both had `Object.keys(...).length === 0`, neither matched the custom-prototype guard, and the key-comparison loop had nothing to iterate — so the function fell through to `return true`.

I added a targeted check right after the key-count comparison:

```ts
if (
  keys1.length === 0 &&
  keys2.length === 0 &&
  Array.isArray(object1) !== Array.isArray(object2)
) {
  return false;
}
```

This returns `false` only when both values are empty *and* one is an array while the other isn't — covering `deepEqual([], {})` / `deepEqual({}, [])` and the nested `{ items: [] }` vs `{ items: {} }` cases (which recurse into this same comparison), in both argument orders.

I deliberately scoped it to the empty case (`keys.length === 0`) so it does **not** break the existing behavior where a non-empty sparse array is considered equal to a plain object with matching numeric-string keys (test `#13346`). All 11 existing tests pass, and no test files were modified.

